"Shell Damage - RC Layered Shell Section (Moment-Curvature with Concrete Damage)
'<hr />
'<h3>Model</h3>
'A reinforced-concrete shell/plate section (per metre of width) is analysed by
'<b>layers</b> (layered shell section). Each concrete layer follows the same
'uniaxial <b>damaged elasticity</b> law as the <i>Concrete Damage</i> example:
#math 360 44
\sigma(\varepsilon) = \bigl(1 - d(\varepsilon)\bigr)\,E\,\varepsilon,\qquad d \in [0,1]
#end math
'As the curvature grows, the compression fibres accumulate damage and the section
'<b>softens</b> — the moment rises to a peak and then descends. This softening of
'the layered shell is what we call <b>shell damage</b>.
'Plane-section kinematics: the strain varies linearly through the thickness,
#math 360 24
\varepsilon(z) = \varepsilon_0 + \kappa\,z
#end math
'where <var>ε_0</var> is the mid-surface strain and <var>κ</var> the curvature.
'Sign convention: tension <var>ε</var> &gt; 0, compression <var>ε</var> &lt; 0.

'<h3>Material properties (concrete = same as Concrete Damage)</h3>
'<table><tr><td>
'Elastic modulus -'E = 30000
'Compressive strength -'f_c = 30
'Peak compressive strain -'ε_c0 = 0.002
'Ultimate compressive strain -'ε_cu = 0.0035
'Tensile strength -'f_t = 3
'Tensile cracking strain -'ε_t0 = f_t/E
'Tension softening strain -'ε_tf = 0.0005
'</td></tr></table>
'Compression softening slope (residual 0.2·f_c reached at ε_cu):
Z = (1 - 0.2)/(ε_cu - ε_c0)
'Reinforcing steel (elasto-plastic):
'Steel modulus -'E_s = 200000
'Yield strength -'f_y = 420

#hide
'--- Constitutive laws σ(ε) [MPa] ---
'Tension: elastic up to ε_t0, then exponential softening
σ_t(ε) = if(ε ≤ ε_t0; E*ε; f_t*exp(-(ε - ε_t0)/ε_tf))
'Compression (x = -ε is the magnitude): Hognestad/Kent-Park parabola then linear softening
σ_c(ε) = if(-ε ≤ ε_c0; -f_c*(2*(-ε/ε_c0) - (-ε/ε_c0)^2); -f_c*max(0.2; 1 - Z*(-ε - ε_c0)))
'Concrete law by sign of ε
σ(ε) = if(ε ≥ 0; σ_t(ε); σ_c(ε))
'Steel: bilinear elasto-plastic
σ_s(ε) = min(f_y; max(-f_y; E_s*ε))
'Scalar concrete damage: d = clamp(1 - σ/(E·ε), 0, 1), with d = 0 at ε = 0
d(ε) = if(ε ≡ 0; 0; min(1; max(0; 1 - σ(ε)/(E*ε))))
#show

'<h3>Section (per metre of width)</h3>
'<table><tr><td>
'Thickness -'t = 200
'Width -'b = 1000
'Number of concrete layers -'nc = 20
'Layer thickness -'dz = t/nc
'Steel area per face -'A_s = 1000
'Cover -'cover = 25
'Steel lever arm -'z_s = t/2 - cover
'</td></tr></table>
'Concrete layer <var>i</var> is centred at:
#math 360 24
z_i = -t/2 + dz\,(i - 0.5),\qquad i = 1 \ldots nc
#end math
zc(i) = -t/2 + dz*(i - 0.5)

'<h3>Section resultants at a given curvature κ</h3>
'Axial force and bending moment are sums over the concrete layers plus the two
'steel layers (at +z_s and -z_s). They depend on the mid-surface strain e and on
'the current curvature κ:
#hide
N(e) = $Sum{σ(e + κ*zc(i))*b*dz @ i = 1 : nc} + σ_s(e + κ*z_s)*A_s + σ_s(e - κ*z_s)*A_s
Mn(e) = $Sum{σ(e + κ*zc(i))*b*dz*zc(i) @ i = 1 : nc} + σ_s(e + κ*z_s)*A_s*z_s + σ_s(e - κ*z_s)*A_s*(-z_s)
#show
#math 360 30
N(e) = \sum_i \sigma(e + \kappa z_i)\,b\,dz + \sigma_s(e+\kappa z_s)A_s + \sigma_s(e-\kappa z_s)A_s
#end math
'For every curvature we first find the mid-surface strain <var>ε_0</var> that makes
'the section <b>axially balanced</b> N(ε_0) = 0 (pure bending), using the built-in
'root finder over ε_0 ∈ [-0.01, 0.01]:
#math 360 24
\varepsilon_0 : \; N(\varepsilon_0) = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; M(\kappa) = M_n(\varepsilon_0)
#end math
'The compression-fibre damage is read at the most compressed fibre (z = -t/2):
#math 360 24
d_{comp} = d\bigl(\varepsilon_0 - \kappa\,t/2\bigr)
#end math

"Reference table (validated)
'The 7 curvatures below reproduce the validated moment-curvature response. For each
'one, ε_0 is solved from N(ε_0)=0 and then M and d_comp are evaluated.
n_ref = 7
κ_ref = [0.000005; 0.00001; 0.000015; 0.0000164; 0.00002; 0.00003; 0.00004]
M_ref = vector(n_ref)
d_ref = vector(n_ref)
#hide
#for j = 1 : n_ref
    κ = κ_ref.j
    ε_0 = $Root{N(e) @ e = -0.01 : 0.01}
    M_ref.j = Mn(ε_0)/1000000
    d_ref.j = d(ε_0 - κ*t/2)
#loop
#show
'<table style="border-collapse:collapse" border="1" cellpadding="4">
'<tr><th>κ (1/mm)</th><th>M (kN·m/m)</th><th>d_comp</th></tr>
'<tr><td>'κ_ref.1'</td><td>'M_ref.1'</td><td>'d_ref.1'</td></tr>
'<tr><td>'κ_ref.2'</td><td>'M_ref.2'</td><td>'d_ref.2'</td></tr>
'<tr><td>'κ_ref.3'</td><td>'M_ref.3'</td><td>'d_ref.3'</td></tr>
'<tr><td>'κ_ref.4'</td><td>'M_ref.4' (peak)</td><td>'d_ref.4'</td></tr>
'<tr><td>'κ_ref.5'</td><td>'M_ref.5'</td><td>'d_ref.5'</td></tr>
'<tr><td>'κ_ref.6'</td><td>'M_ref.6'</td><td>'d_ref.6'</td></tr>
'<tr><td>'κ_ref.7'</td><td>'M_ref.7'</td><td>'d_ref.7'</td></tr>
'</table>

"Curvature sweep with a loop (builds M-κ and d-κ vectors)
'A #for loop sweeps κ from 0 to 4·10⁻⁵ (1/mm). At every step it solves the axial
'equilibrium for ε_0 and stores the moment and the compression damage in vectors.
nk = 41
κ_max = 0.00004
Δκ = κ_max/(nk - 1)
κv = vector(nk)
Mv = vector(nk)
dv = vector(nk)
#hide
#for k = 1 : nk
    κ = (k - 1)*Δκ
    κv.k = κ
    ε_0 = $Root{N(e) @ e = -0.01 : 0.01}
    Mv.k = Mn(ε_0)/1000000
    dv.k = d(ε_0 - κ*t/2)
#loop
#show
'Peak moment found by the sweep -'max(Mv)' kN·m/m
'Residual moment at κ = 4·10⁻⁵ -'Mv.nk' kN·m/m
'Maximum compression damage -'max(dv)

PlotWidth = 500
PlotHeight = 300
#post
"Moment-curvature  M(κ)
'The section stiffens, reaches a peak moment (~72.3 kN·m/m near κ ≈ 1.64·10⁻⁵) and
'then softens as concrete damage spreads through the compression layers.
$Plot{κv.k|Mv.k @ k = 1 : nk}
"Compression-fibre damage  d_comp(κ)
'Scalar damage at the most compressed fibre grows monotonically with curvature.
$Plot{κv.k|dv.k @ k = 1 : nk}
